# prune-sandbox-images.service — one pass of the Fabro sandbox-image prune
# (see ./prune-sandbox-images.sh for every gate it applies before removing
# anything). Item `livespec-h96p`.
#
# Oneshot rather than a daemon: the work is a bounded pass over one
# repository's image records, so there is nothing to keep resident between
# runs, and a oneshot's exit status is a signal the timer records for free.
#
# `--apply` IS SPELLED OUT HERE, in the open. The script removes nothing
# without it — a bare invocation reports and exits — so an operator reading
# this unit can see exactly which of the two behaviours is scheduled, and
# `systemctl cat prune-sandbox-images.service` is the whole answer to "does
# this thing delete images". Drop the flag and the timer becomes a monitor.
#
# Requires=k3s.service, and that is a SAFETY ordering rather than a
# convenience. The prune's central gate is a cluster-wide read of every
# workload's PodSpec; without the API server it cannot know what is needed, and
# its own fail-closed check would end the run non-zero on every tick. Requiring
# the unit is also what keeps this off an agent node structurally: an agent
# runs `k3s-agent.service`, not `k3s.service`, so this unit cannot start there
# even if the file were installed by hand. The sibling wedged-runner and
# runner-pod-lifecycle scans are ordered the same way for the same reason.
#
# KUBECONFIG is set here because a systemd service has no login shell, so the
# cluster credential has to be named explicitly rather than inherited — the
# same line the sibling arc-log-archive unit carries.
[Unit]
Description=Prune surplus Fabro sandbox images from this node's containerd
Documentation=https://github.com/thewoolleyman/livespec-dev-tooling/blob/master/ci-runner/k3s/phase2/warm-cache/README.md
Requires=k3s.service
After=k3s.service network-online.target

[Service]
Type=oneshot
Environment=KUBECONFIG=/etc/rancher/k3s/k3s.yaml
ExecStart=/usr/local/lib/ci-runner-k3s/prune-sandbox-images.sh --apply
